1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:06,500
Have you ever wondered why it takes like three different government departments six whole months to update your home address?

2
00:00:06,780 --> 00:00:10,890
Right. Or why a simple request just seems to vanish into this administrative black hole.

3
00:00:11,040 --> 00:00:18,180
Exactly. So today, our mission for this deep dive is to decode these massive, complex organizations.

4
00:00:18,840 --> 00:00:26,240
We're going to explore how you can read any government ministry through the four layers of enterprise architecture, or, uh, EA.

5
00:00:26,540 --> 00:00:32,729
Yeah. The goal is to give you the tools so you can understand exactly how they function and, you know, precisely where they break down.

6
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:41,650
Right. So to figure out why an address update takes so long, I mean, my first instinct is to just pull up the ministry's organizational chart- Good... and see who's in charge of what.

7
00:00:41,900 --> 00:00:45,400
Well, that is actually the exact mistake first-time architects make.

8
00:00:45,460 --> 00:00:49,530
Really? Yeah. An organizational chart, um, it only tells you who reports to whom.

9
00:00:49,540 --> 00:00:51,680
It does not tell you what the body actually does.

10
00:00:51,760 --> 00:00:52,120
Oh, I see.

11
00:00:52,340 --> 00:00:59,900
So the first layer of EA is the business layer, and to understand it, your true deliverable is a capability map and a service catalog.

12
00:01:00,600 --> 00:01:02,760
Wait, what exactly is a capability in this context?

13
00:01:03,160 --> 00:01:12,920
So a capability is just a fundamental action the body can take, like, um, registering a learner, and a service is how a citizen actually

14
00:01:12,980 --> 00:01:14,200
receives that capability.

15
00:01:14,260 --> 00:01:18,920
Okay, so if you just map the boxes on an org chart, you're really just mapping the bureaucracy.

16
00:01:18,980 --> 00:01:20,340
Exactly, not the actual work.

17
00:01:20,610 --> 00:01:25,760
But, you know, knowing what a ministry does is kind of useless if you don't know what information they're using to do it.

18
00:01:25,900 --> 00:01:30,620
No. 'Cause bureaucratic structures will change, and technology cycles out constantly.

19
00:01:30,700 --> 00:01:33,980
Oh, absolutely. But the actual citizen records remain.

20
00:01:34,680 --> 00:01:39,160
It seems to me that data is really the only permanent fixture in an organization.

21
00:01:39,200 --> 00:01:43,130
Precisely. The data layer outlasts every single application above it.

22
00:01:43,760 --> 00:01:48,420
So the essential deliverable for the second layer is, uh, a data domain catalog.

23
00:01:48,460 --> 00:01:49,840
A data domain, like a category.

24
00:01:49,900 --> 00:01:54,960
Yeah, a category of information the government agrees on, such as a person or a payment.

25
00:01:54,970 --> 00:01:59,690
The key here is that you must identify exactly one authoritative owner per domain.

26
00:01:59,800 --> 00:02:03,800
Because if you lack that authoritative owner, you end up with complete fragmentation, right?

27
00:02:03,810 --> 00:02:13,410
Exactly. Like, if the tax office and the motor vehicle department don't agree on the exact definition of a person, you end up with a citizen who, you know, exists to pay

28
00:02:13,460 --> 00:02:16,540
taxes, but somehow doesn't exist when they try to get a driver's license.

29
00:02:16,620 --> 00:02:22,360
That is spot on. The common mistake here is merely listing the databases you can physically see.

30
00:02:22,380 --> 00:02:23,740
Which just documents the mess.

31
00:02:23,880 --> 00:02:26,400
Right. If you do that, you just document the fragmentation.

32
00:02:26,440 --> 00:02:31,560
You end up with, like, five different systems holding conflicting versions of the exact same citizen.

33
00:02:31,640 --> 00:02:37,780
Wow. So the data layer has to sit above the databases to force that single agreed upon owner.

34
00:02:38,060 --> 00:02:46,989
Okay, that makes sense. So if data domains are the raw ingredients like flour and sugar, then the application layer must be the kitchen appliances processing them.

35
00:02:47,020 --> 00:02:47,900
I like that.

36
00:02:47,910 --> 00:02:52,720
And obviously, a blender is completely useless if you don't know what recipe you're making or what ingredients you're putting into it.

37
00:02:53,020 --> 00:03:01,560
The mechanism there is perfect. Yeah. So this third layer, the application layer, it details the specific software that supports those business capabilities.

38
00:03:01,840 --> 00:03:10,519
But the trap people fall into here is creating a useless software inventory, just, you know, a list of software names and versions.

39
00:03:10,680 --> 00:03:14,960
Which just tells you a system exists, but not why it's there in the first place.

40
00:03:15,320 --> 00:03:18,859
Exactly. Instead, you need an application portfolio.

41
00:03:18,870 --> 00:03:23,260
That portfolio connects every single system back to the specific capability it serves.

42
00:03:23,320 --> 00:03:25,760
And the exact data domain it touches?

43
00:03:26,060 --> 00:03:31,910
Yes. Without those connections, you literally just have a list of IT assets, not an architecture.

44
00:03:32,269 --> 00:03:34,010
Wait, but what about the physical hardware?

45
00:03:34,580 --> 00:03:38,820
I mean, if the servers crash, the whole ministry goes offline, and no one gets their driver's license anyway.

46
00:03:38,840 --> 00:03:40,060
Well, yeah, that's true.

47
00:03:40,080 --> 00:03:45,160
So shouldn't the hardware and networks be the very first thing we map out to ensure the foundation is secure?

48
00:03:45,200 --> 00:03:50,020
It's a very tempting starting point, but going too deep too early is a massive trap.

49
00:03:50,080 --> 00:03:52,790
This brings us to the fourth tier, the technology layer.

50
00:03:52,960 --> 00:03:54,980
Okay, and that covers what exactly?

51
00:03:55,020 --> 00:03:57,820
It covers networks, hosting, and security controls.

52
00:03:57,860 --> 00:04:06,120
But first-time architects often attempt this massive server-by-server audit before they even understand a single business capability.

53
00:04:06,220 --> 00:04:11,380
Oh, wow. So they spend months mapping cables for a system that might not even be necessary.

54
00:04:11,480 --> 00:04:16,440
Exactly. That detailed server audit belongs to the operations team, not the architects.

55
00:04:17,079 --> 00:04:21,940
At the architecture level, your deliverable should simply be a short list of technology standards.

56
00:04:22,070 --> 00:04:26,160
And I guess a high-level infrastructure picture that just points out single points of failure.

57
00:04:26,280 --> 00:04:32,720
Spot on. Okay, so we have the capabilities, the data, the applications, and the technology foundation, and they just rely on each other completely.

58
00:04:32,780 --> 00:04:35,740
They do. These four layers are not separate documents.

59
00:04:35,800 --> 00:04:45,680
They connect vertically. Right. Every public service traces down to a business capability, which is served by an application, which uses a data domain, which runs on the technology layer.

60
00:04:46,240 --> 00:04:55,100
So when you can trace a service seamlessly from the citizen down to the infrastructure, you're finally reading the government as a cohesive system.

61
00:04:55,330 --> 00:05:02,600
Precisely. If you find a layer that floats free, um, like an application that doesn't connect up to a capability or down to a server,

62
00:05:03,420 --> 00:05:05,520
you have identified a critical gap.

63
00:05:05,740 --> 00:05:07,910
It's all about following the thread from top to bottom,

